WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:07.069
In "Star Trek Picard" season 2, episode 4,
we finally learn what the deal is with Picard's

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:12.240
accent, spot a reference that's been with
Trek for more than 50 years, and we also learn

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.120
that Guinan stays strapped, no matter what
century she's in.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.210
In the fourth episode, "Watcher," Picard and
Jurati leave the confines of the La Sirena

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.010
and head to the Picard Chateau in 2024.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.130
"Oh dear."

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.150
"So Chateau Picard is abandoned in this era."

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.940
"Nearly a century at this point."

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:37.010
The former Enterprise captain explains that
at this point in history -- nearly 400 years

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.320
in the past -- the Picard vineyard has been
abandoned for generations, and it will be

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.780
decades before the Picards return to occupy
it once again.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.309
While giving Jurati a brief tour, Picard finally
resolves a debate regarding his backstory

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:53.489
that has raged among Trekkies for more than
three decades.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:58.309
According to Picard, during the Second World
War, Nazis occupying France used his family's

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:00.290
chateau as a base of operations.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:04.890
His family only survived, he says, when they
fled into the underground tunnels.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.190
From there, the family escaped across the
English Channel to the United Kingdom.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:14.900
This is a long-awaited explanation as to why
Jean-Luc Picard, a Frenchman, is played by

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.950
the English actor Patrick Stewart with no
discernible French accent.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:23.340
It sounds like after arriving in the United
Kingdom, the Picards assimilated into English

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.830
culture, keeping their British accents even
after returning to France more than a century

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.520
later.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.841
Having brought the Borg Queen aboard the La
Sirena to help guide them back in time, Picard

00:01:33.841 --> 00:01:37.520
and his crew once again rely on her for interdimensional
knowledge.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.450
The Borg Queen claims to know the location
of the Watcher, a being with awareness of

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.180
other timelines which might be able to help
them.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:49.020
However, to access the information, Jurati
needs to be partially assimilated.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.020
It's a risky procedure, but she convinces
Picard it's the only way.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.259
They get some of the information they need,
but much of it is scrambled in Jurati's head.

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:01.900
While Picard gives her a tour of his family's
French chateau, Jurati begins subconsciously

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.409
fixating on the number 15.

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:09.689
Picard points out that while in the chateau's
library she moves 15 beads on an abacus, selects

00:02:09.689 --> 00:02:15.859
the 15th volume of an encyclopedia, and picks
up a bottle of pinot noir from 1915.

00:02:15.859 --> 00:02:21.209
This seems to indicate that there's some information
rattling around in her subconscious -- something

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:25.930
Picard would recognize, because he's seen
this sort of thing before.

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:30.799
The subconscious fixation feels like a direct
nod to the events of "The Next Generation"

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:31.940
episode "Cause And Effect."

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.409
In that episode, the Enterprise is stuck in
a repeating loop of time, and Data implants

00:02:36.409 --> 00:02:41.579
a subconscious message to himself regarding
the number three to help them solve the dilemma.

00:02:41.579 --> 00:02:46.180
Perhaps thanks to this experience, Picard
can recognize the same phenomenon in Jurati

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:51.799
and see the fixation on 15 as a potentially
helpful subconscious message.

00:02:51.799 --> 00:02:56.529
Time travel is at the core of some of the
most classic "Star Trek" stories and provides

00:02:56.529 --> 00:02:59.659
some of the franchise's most memorable moments.

00:02:59.659 --> 00:03:04.569
One such instance is from "Star Trek IV: The
Voyage Home," which sees Kirk and Spock aboard

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:07.379
a commuter bus after traveling back in time
to San Francisco.

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:13.909
There, a large mohawk-sporting punk is carrying
a boom box and blaring loud music, specifically

00:03:13.909 --> 00:03:18.669
the song "I Hate You" written and performed
by "Star Trek" producer Kirk Thatcher.

00:03:18.669 --> 00:03:23.760
Annoyed by the crass music and cacophony of
disruptive noise, Kirk asks the punk to turn

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.349
it down, only to be met with a middle finger.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:31.489
Seeing no other choice, Spock reaches forward
and gives the man a Vulcan neck pinch, knocking

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:33.720
him unconscious and turning off the music.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:39.010
In the "Picard" episode "Watcher," we get
a repeat of this scene, but with far different

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.010
results.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:44.109
While Seven and Raffi travel on a similar
commuter bus in Los Angeles, another mohawked

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:49.421
punk carries a boom box playing a slightly
altered new version of the same song, "I Still

00:03:49.421 --> 00:03:50.421
Hate You."

00:03:50.421 --> 00:03:54.909
This time though, when Seven angrily asks
the man to turn the music off, the punk goes

00:03:54.909 --> 00:04:00.260
from angry to ashamed, turning off his boom
box before sheepishly apologizing for causing

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.519
the pair any trouble.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:04.159
"I just like that song.

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:05.959
Sorry.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:07.760
Sorry."

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.889
Seeking the Watcher, Picard finally gets a
set of coordinates and beams over, finding

00:04:11.889 --> 00:04:13.629
himself in a familiar alley.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:19.340
He materializes at 10 Forward Ave, the same
location as Guinan's bar in the 25th century,

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.289
and realizes that Guinan may be the person
he's looking for.

00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:26.909
However, it's a much smaller detail that's
sure to draw the eyes of longtime Trekkies,

00:04:26.909 --> 00:04:32.060
as a brick wall in the background holds a
well-worn "Star Trek" prop: a poster for a

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.780
boxing match at Madison Square Garden.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:38.250
First appearing in the classic "Star Trek"
episode "The City on the Edge of Forever,"

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.120
the poster has since become an iconic Easter
egg across the franchise, making re-appearances

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:44.930
in other time travel episodes.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:49.061
It turned up in the background of "Past Tense,"
a two-part story on "Deep Space Nine," when

00:04:49.061 --> 00:04:53.490
O'Brien and Kira travel back to the 20th century
looking for Sisko, Dax, and Bashir.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:58.050
The poster was seen again, just barely, in
the "Star Trek: Enterprise" episode "Storm

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:01.930
Front" when history is altered and Captain
Archer finds himself in Nazi-occupied New

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.039
York City in 1944.

00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:09.640
Whether it was left up on the wall for decades
or is some sort of 21st-century rematch, we

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.810
still have to wonder why they're advertising
a boxing match occurring at Madison Square

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:17.610
Garden in downtown Los Angeles.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:22.509
Entering the bar, Picard discovers a much
younger Guinan -- now played by actress Ito

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:25.930
Aghayere -- with a much different attitude.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:31.320
Far more cynical, this Guinan in 2024 has
had enough of the human race and is about

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.400
to close her bar and depart planet Earth.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:36.860
She also doesn't recognize Picard, and that
makes sense.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:41.330
After Q altered history, there was no longer
a starship Enterprise to travel back in time

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:47.620
to 1893, and allow Picard and company to first
meet Guinan in the "TNG" episode "Time's Arrow."

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:52.789
It's possible that her new, less hopeful attitude
is the result of never having met Picard and

00:05:52.789 --> 00:05:55.730
getting a peek into humanity's -- and her
own -- future.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.120
"If you're here to drink, you're late.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.949
If you're here to loot, you're early."

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:06.499
When she meets Picard in "Watcher," Guinan
is more than just cynical -- she's distrusting

00:06:06.499 --> 00:06:08.130
and suspicious.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:13.590
She makes it known she won't tolerate a thief
by pulling a shotgun out from under the bar.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.729
This should come as no surprise to fans of
"The Next Generation," who will remember that

00:06:17.729 --> 00:06:21.259
Guinan is known for keeping a powerful firearm
at her side.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:24.789
In the episode "Night Terrors," when fear
grips the Enterprise and a fight breaks out

00:06:24.789 --> 00:06:28.599
in the ship's lounge, Guinan pulls a phaser
rifle from under her bar.

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:34.409
But in 2024, a simple pump-action shotgun
is all she needs to ward off thieves, brawlers,

00:06:34.409 --> 00:06:35.409
and malcontents.

00:06:35.409 --> 00:06:42.539
"Your suspicions, Madame Guinan, are undoubtedly
based upon your keen observational skills."

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:47.439
Viewers still reeling from the surprising
sight of a younger Guinan and a new actress

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:50.850
playing her may have missed the next Easter
egg.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:54.530
Walking through the streets of downtown L.A.,
Guinan is headed to donate some items from

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.090
her bar, which she has decided to close down
before she leaves the planet.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:01.849
She and Picard stop at a tent labeled 21st
Street Mission Donation Center, where they

00:07:01.849 --> 00:07:05.090
drop off Guinan's donations to benefit the
less fortunate.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:10.600
Of course, the 21st Street Mission is nothing
new in "Star Trek," as it was previously seen

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.590
in the episode "City on the Edge of Forever."

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:17.779
When Kirk and Spock travel back to 1930s New
York, they're aided by Edith Keeler, who runs

00:07:17.779 --> 00:07:24.479
the 21st Street Mission, a homeless shelter
that houses, feeds, and clothes those in need.

00:07:24.479 --> 00:07:28.169
"Excuse me, Miss.

00:07:28.169 --> 00:07:29.169
Where are we?"

00:07:29.169 --> 00:07:31.879
"You're in the 21st Street Mission."

00:07:31.879 --> 00:07:37.219
At the tail end of the fourth episode, "Watcher,"
we find Q sitting at a table outdoors reading

00:07:37.219 --> 00:07:41.240
a copy of a contemporary newspaper, the Los
Angeles Times.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:46.930
In this version of 2024, it seems the world
has the same issues as the real world in 2022,

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.789
with one headline noting that "Brynner fights
unionization."

00:07:50.789 --> 00:07:54.650
The headline is a reference to Chris Brynner,
the wealthy magnate from the "Star Trek: Deep

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:57.539
Space Nine" time travel episode "Past Tense."

00:07:57.539 --> 00:08:02.020
There he appeared as something of a cross
between Bill Gates and Rupert Murdoch.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:06.999
Brynner is the owner and CEO of a tech conglomerate
called Brynner Information Systems and wields

00:08:06.999 --> 00:08:10.960
political influence through the several network
channels he controls.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.879
While he presents himself as an elitist, he
seems genuinely good-hearted and helps Dax

00:08:15.879 --> 00:08:20.410
locate her friends in the Sanctuary District,
even coming to question the ethics of his

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:21.410
time.

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:25.159
However, the headline of the paper held by
Q reveals that Brynner is now anti-union,

00:08:25.159 --> 00:08:30.590
suggesting that he has more in common with
the corporate ethics of his era than we previously

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.200
thought.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:37.270
As Q sits at an outdoor dining area reading
the Los Angeles Times, a young woman comes

00:08:37.270 --> 00:08:41.120
walking out of the NASA facility where they
are preparing for the upcoming space flight

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.120
mission to Europa.

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:47.970
The building is marked conspicuously as the
Jackson Roykirk Plaza, which at first might

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.170
seem like the name of a real-life landmark
in Los Angeles.

00:08:51.170 --> 00:08:56.389
However, fans of "Star Trek: The Original
Series" will spot the name right away as a

00:08:56.389 --> 00:08:59.839
reference to a character mentioned in the
episode "The Changeling."

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:04.440
In that episode, Captain Kirk and the Enterprise
encounter a long-lost Earth space probe called

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.130
Nomad that was launched in 2002.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:13.100
The probe, which was designed by a brilliant
21st-century scientist named Jackson Roykirk,

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:17.579
was piloted by an artificial intelligence
given a mission to seek out new life in the

00:09:17.579 --> 00:09:18.579
galaxy.

00:09:18.579 --> 00:09:23.079
Unfortunately, the probe's logic systems identified
the aliens it encountered as imperfect and

00:09:23.079 --> 00:09:24.529
turned against them.

00:09:24.529 --> 00:09:30.000
“This is the creator of Nomad – perhaps
the most brilliant, though erratic scientist

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.230
of his time."

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:36.090
It seems that, in the fictional 2024 seen
on "Picard," Roykirk is still remembered as

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:38.490
one of the space agency's leading minds.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:42.779
Whether he is still alive and working for
them in his later years or the plaza was named

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:46.990
as a memorial to him after his death remains
unknown.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:52.019
Speaking of that last scene, it's not just
any book the woman is leafing through -- it's

00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:54.850
a book titled "The Pallid Son" by author Tracy
Tormé.

00:09:54.850 --> 00:10:00.769
A sharp eye will note that the book cover
is adorned with the banner "A Dixon Hill Mystery."

00:10:00.769 --> 00:10:05.590
Dixon Hill was the name of a fictional detective
in "Star Trek" and star of several novels

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:09.440
-- and later holodeck programs -- preferred
by Jean-Luc Picard.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:12.880
First mentioned in "The Next Generation,"
Dixon Hill has been referenced over the years

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.209
in episodes of "Enterprise" and "Voyager"
as well.

00:10:16.209 --> 00:10:21.399
"I think that your marvelous mind is trying
to tell us something."

00:10:21.399 --> 00:10:23.880
"Look at you, Dixon Hill."

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:28.150
The book's fictional author, Tracy Tormé,
is named after the real-life writer of "The

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:32.490
Next Generation" episode that the detective
series first appeared in, "The Big Goodbye."

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.890
"Ready for San Francisco, Mr. Whalen?"

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.720
"More than ready, sir."

